<p class="gna-lede">If your jaw clicks, aches or feels stiff in the morning, the first question most people ask is whether they will need an operation. For the large majority, the answer is no. A well-made splint, combined with simple self-care, settles most temporomandibular disorders (TMD). Surgery has a role — but a narrow and specific one.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Start with a splint and conservative care. Consider a procedure only when one of three things is true: the jaw is <strong>locked</strong> and cannot open normally, there is <strong>structural joint disease</strong> confirmed on imaging (such as advanced arthritis or a displaced disc that will not reduce), or <strong>several months of well-run conservative treatment</strong> have not controlled your pain or function.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Most TMJ pain comes from the chewing muscles, not the joint itself — and muscle pain responds well to non-surgical care.</li>
    <li>A splint reduces load on the joint and muscles; it is not a cure for a clicking sound.</li>
    <li>Minimally invasive options (arthrocentesis, arthroscopy) sit between a splint and open surgery.</li>
    <li>Open joint surgery or joint replacement is reserved for a small group with severe structural damage.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="how-splints-work">How a TMJ splint works</h2>
<p>A TMJ splint (also called an occlusal splint or stabilisation appliance) is a thin, hard acrylic plate that fits over the upper or lower teeth. It is made from an impression or digital scan of your mouth and adjusted so that your back teeth meet it evenly. This does three useful things:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Spreads biting forces</strong> evenly, so no single tooth or one side of the jaw takes the full load during night-time clenching.</li>
  <li><strong>Relaxes the chewing muscles</strong> (masseter and temporalis), which are the source of pain in most patients.</li>
  <li><strong>Protects the teeth</strong> from wear if you grind (bruxism).</li>
</ul>
<p>Splints are usually worn at night. Most people notice a difference within four to eight weeks, provided the splint is adjusted at follow-up visits and combined with other measures: a softer diet during flare-ups, avoiding gum chewing and nail biting, warm compresses, gentle jaw exercises taught by a physiotherapist, and short courses of anti-inflammatory medication when your doctor advises it.</p>
<div class="gna-callout">
  <p><strong>Splint or night guard?</strong> Soft, over-the-counter "boil-and-bite" guards are designed to protect teeth during sport. They are not a substitute for a custom TMJ splint and can occasionally increase clenching.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="when-splint-is-enough">When a splint is usually enough</h2>
<p>Conservative care is the right first step — and very often the only step — if you have:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Aching in the cheeks or temples that is worse in the morning or after stress</li>
  <li>A jaw that clicks but opens fully and is not painful, or is only mildly painful</li>
  <li>Headaches linked to clenching or grinding</li>
  <li>Tenderness of the chewing muscles with normal joint imaging</li>
</ul>
<p>In these situations surgery rarely adds anything, and the risks of an operation are not justified. Our guide on <a href="/blogs/what-is-tmj-disorder/">what TMJ disorder actually is</a> explains why muscle pain behaves so differently from joint disease.</p>

<h2 id="getting-the-most">Getting the most from a splint</h2>
<p>A splint is a tool, not a treatment on its own. People who do well with one usually combine it with a few simple habits:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wear it consistently.</strong> Most splints are worn every night. Occasional use rarely helps, because clenching and grinding happen night after night.</li>
  <li><strong>Come back for adjustments.</strong> The fit is checked and the biting surface fine-tuned at follow-up visits, typically at two to six weeks. A splint that rocks, presses on one tooth or makes your bite feel "off" in the morning needs adjusting — do not trim it yourself.</li>
  <li><strong>Notice daytime clenching.</strong> Many people clench while concentrating, driving or using a phone. Resting position is "lips together, teeth apart, tongue resting behind the upper front teeth". Small reminders through the day help retrain the habit.</li>
  <li><strong>Look after sleep and stress.</strong> Poor sleep and stress both increase muscle tension. If you snore loudly or wake unrefreshed, mention it — <a href="/blogs/snoring-vs-sleep-apnea-difference/">sleep-disordered breathing</a> and night-time clenching are often linked.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep it clean.</strong> Brush the splint with cold water and a soft brush, and store it dry in its case. Hot water can warp acrylic.</li>
</ul>
<p>If pain increases after starting a splint, or your bite changes, stop wearing it and contact the clinic. That is uncommon, but it means the design or fit needs to be reviewed.</p>

