<h2 id="when-procedures-help">When a procedure is worth discussing</h2>
<p>A specialist will usually talk to you about a procedure in these circumstances:</p>
<h3>1. Closed lock</h3>
<p>If the disc inside the joint has slipped forward and stays there, the jaw can suddenly stop opening beyond two finger-widths. This is called a closed lock. When it does not release with manipulation and conservative care, <strong>arthrocentesis</strong> — washing the joint with sterile fluid through two fine needles under local or general anaesthesia — frequently improves opening and pain.</p>
<h3>2. Persistent pain with joint changes on imaging</h3>
<p>When an MRI or CBCT scan shows inflammation, adhesions or early degenerative change, and several months of good conservative care have not helped, <strong>TMJ arthroscopy</strong> allows the surgeon to look inside the joint with a small camera, release adhesions and treat inflamed tissue. Read more about <a href="/tmj-arthroscopy">TMJ arthroscopy at Gnathos Facial</a>.</p>
<h3>3. Severe structural disease</h3>
<p>Advanced arthritis, ankylosis (fusion of the joint) or damage after injury may need <strong>open joint surgery</strong> or, rarely, total joint replacement. These are major operations and are planned with detailed 3D imaging.</p>
<h3>4. A bite or jaw-position problem driving the symptoms</h3>
<p>Occasionally a significant skeletal imbalance between the upper and lower jaws contributes to joint overload. In carefully selected patients, <a href="/jaw-surgery">corrective jaw surgery</a> is considered — but never as a first-line treatment for pain alone.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-ladder">The treatment ladder at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What it involves</th><th>Typically considered when</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Self-care &amp; physiotherapy</td><td>Soft diet in flare-ups, posture and stress measures, jaw exercises</td><td>All patients, from day one</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Custom splint</td><td>Night-time acrylic appliance, adjusted at reviews</td><td>Muscle pain, clenching, painful clicking</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Medication / injections</td><td>Short-course anti-inflammatories; selected joint or muscle injections</td><td>Flare-ups, or as an adjunct to the splint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Arthrocentesis</td><td>Joint wash-out through two needles</td><td>Closed lock, inflamed joint not settling</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Arthroscopy</td><td>Keyhole examination and treatment of the joint</td><td>Persistent joint pain with imaging changes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Open surgery / replacement</td><td>Joint repair, reconstruction or prosthesis</td><td>Ankylosis, severe arthritis, failed earlier treatment</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="risks-recovery">Risks and recovery of TMJ procedures</h2>
<p>Every step up the ladder brings more potential benefit for the right patient — and more potential for side effects. It helps to know what each involves before deciding.</p>
<h3>Arthrocentesis</h3>
<p>Usually done as a day procedure. Expect swelling and soreness in front of the ear for a few days, a soft diet for about a week, and jaw exercises afterwards to maintain the opening gained. Temporary numbness or weakness around the ear and a temporary change in the bite can occur; serious complications are uncommon. Read more in our guide to <a href="/blogs/tmj-arthrocentesis-explained/">TMJ arthrocentesis</a>.</p>
<h3>Arthroscopy</h3>
<p>Performed under general anaesthesia, typically as day-care or with an overnight stay. Most people return to desk work within one to two weeks, with physiotherapy continuing for several weeks. Possible risks include swelling, temporary facial-nerve weakness, ear symptoms and, rarely, injury to nearby structures. See our <a href="/blogs/tmj-arthroscopy-recovery-what-to-expect/">week-by-week arthroscopy recovery guide</a>.</p>
<h3>Open joint surgery and joint replacement</h3>
<p>These are major operations with a longer recovery — often several weeks before normal eating — and a higher risk profile. They are considered only when the joint is structurally severely damaged and simpler measures cannot help.</p>
<p>Whatever is recommended, results vary from person to person, and improvement is usually gradual rather than immediate.</p>

<h2 id="red-flags">When to be seen sooner</h2>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Seek prompt assessment if you notice:</strong> a jaw that has suddenly locked open or closed; swelling, fever or pus near the jaw; numbness of the lip or chin; a bite that changed after an injury; or jaw pain with chest pain or breathlessness (call 108 for the last). These are not typical TMD symptoms and need examination.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="how-we-decide">How we decide in clinic</h2>
<p>At your first consultation we take a detailed history, examine the joints and chewing muscles, measure your mouth opening and check your bite. Imaging is ordered only when it will change the plan. We then agree a step-by-step programme with clear review points — for example, a splint review at six weeks and a decision point at three months.</p>
<p>If you do need a procedure, you will receive a written explanation of what it involves, the expected recovery, the realistic benefits and the risks, so that you can make an informed choice. Procedures are carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad.</p>
